diff --git a/docs/docs/advanced_features/hyperparameter_tuning.mdx b/docs/docs/advanced_features/hyperparameter_tuning.mdx
index db3952a872..0f5427b49e 100644
--- a/docs/docs/advanced_features/hyperparameter_tuning.mdx
+++ b/docs/docs/advanced_features/hyperparameter_tuning.mdx
@@ -80,3 +80,59 @@ Data parallelism is better for throughput. When there is enough GPU memory, alwa
 - Try other quantization (e.g. FP8 quantization with `--quantization fp8`)
 - Try other parallelism strategies (e.g. [expert parallelism](https://lmsys.org/blog/2025-05-05-large-scale-ep/)) or DP attention for deepseek models (with `--attn-dp-size 8`).
 - If the workload has many shared prefixes, try `--schedule-policy lpm`. Here, `lpm` stands for longest prefix match. It reorders requests to encourage more cache hits but introduces more scheduling overhead.
+
+### Conditional short-prefill protection
+
+A continuing chunked prefill normally consumes the chunk-token budget before
+waiting requests are considered. To reserve some of that budget when the
+current queue head has a small uncached prompt, enable
+`--short-prefill-token-threshold`. For example:
+
+```bash
+--chunked-prefill-size 16384 \
+--short-prefill-token-threshold 2048 \
+--short-prefill-chunk-size 4096 \
+--short-prefill-max-tokens 6144
+```
+
+The default threshold is zero (disabled). With the example profile, an eligible
+queue head caps the continuing chunk at 4096 tokens and the total chunk-token
+budget at 6144 for that scheduling pass. Otherwise, the original chunk budget
+applies. Queue ordering is unchanged; a shorter request behind an ineligible
+head does not bypass it.
+
+The queue head's uncached size is its prompt length minus its cached prefix.
+Cache-aware policies already match every waiting request against the radix
+cache; under a cache-agnostic policy such as `fcfs` over a cache without a fast
+match, the head alone is matched for this test, so a long, mostly cached prompt
+whose uncached suffix fits the threshold qualifies too.
+`--short-prefill-max-tokens` cannot exceed `--chunked-prefill-size`: the
+protected pass is carved out of the chunk budget, never added to it.
+
+By default only the queue head decides, so one long, uncached request at the
+head keeps every short request behind it waiting until the continuing prompt
+has been fully prefilled: only one request may continue chunking, so the head
+cannot start, and the pass ends there. `--short-prefill-lookahead K` (0, the
+default, looks at the head alone) examines the first `K` waiting requests in
+queue order instead, each matched against the prefix cache at most once, and
+the first eligible one caps the continuing chunk. While a chunk continues, a
+request among those `K` that would have to be truncated is stepped over rather
+than ending the pass, so later requests that fit the remaining budget are still
+admitted, in queue order. A request that is stepped over keeps its place and is
+served when the continuing prompt finishes or the budget allows. Any other
+refusal (KV memory, request slots, a preemption made for that request) still
+ends the pass. The cost is up to `K` extra prefix matches per protected pass,
+and the continuing prompt is capped more often, since any of the `K` requests,
+not just the head, can make a pass protected.
+
+This uses the scheduler's existing compute-budget units, page-rounded token
+counts. KV page slack and other memory limits remain separate admission
+checks. A matching threshold does
+not guarantee admission when KV, request slots, alignment or another budget
+prevents the request from fitting. Only one request may continue chunking into
+the next pass.
+
+This option requires static chunked prefill and does not apply to diffusion
+language-model batching. Smaller protected passes can reduce throughput or
+increase the long request's completion latency. Validate TTFT and throughput
+for the intended workload; this option does not promise a latency improvement.
