+"""The chat handler's ``input_ids`` path, fed the router's ids, builds the request the
+messages path builds (part 0033, #8132, for the serving router #8131).
+
+the serving router renders and encodes every DeepSeek-V4.1 chat prompt itself before it
+dispatches (its exact count). #8131 sends those ids as the chat body's ``input_ids``
+beside the unchanged ``messages``, so the engine skips its own render and
+``tokenizer.encode`` (#8125: ~98 % of box-c's 232 ms mean chat-preprocess run). Stock
+SGLang's chat ``input_ids`` path (``ChatCompletionRequest.input_ids``) is on box-c's tree:
+``_process_messages`` still builds the tool-call constraint and the reasoning decision
+from ``tools`` / ``tool_choice`` / ``chat_template_kwargs``, and only the render and the
+encode are skipped. This file proves, CPU-only, that for the router's own bodies the
+``GenerateReqInput`` that reaches the tokenizer manager is the same either way.
+
+The fixture (``fixtures/chat_input_ids_dsv41.jsonl.gz``, minted by the router repo's
+``sglang-patches/b4da0c4b/input_ids/``) holds, per request, the router's SGLang egress
+body and its exact prompt ids, both produced by the router's own code at the router sha in
+its header (the OpenAI facade parse, the counter's DSv4.1 render, the pinned vocabulary's
+segment encode, the SGLang egress encode). The requests are the two recorded box-c output
+corpora (76 bodies box-c answered, each with box-c's own ``usage.prompt_tokens``), every
+tool-call record continued by a turn with tool results (36) and, for the thinking ones, one
+more user turn (8), and 21 single-member variants (``tool_choice`` required / named /
+none, serial calls, ``stop``, content parts, a developer message, a trailing assistant
+turn, no ``chat_template_kwargs``, effort words, logprobs, plain multi-turn chats), and
+26 tool-member null bodies (#8328): every case the engine accepts of the router repo's
+``crates/sbr-wire/tests/fixtures/dsv41-tool-nulls.jsonl`` (nulls at depth 1-4 inside a
+schema, in objects and in lists, ``parameters: {}``, ``{"a": null}``, ``parameters: null``,
+``description: null``, ``defer_loading: null``, ``strict``, floats, non-ASCII text), one
+body each and all in one thinking body, plus a null-schema tool called in the history and
+forced by ``tool_choice`` required and named. For those the fixture also keeps the body as
+the client sent it (``client``): the router posts no ``parameters`` for a null schema
+(the engine's ``exclude_none`` drops it from the render) and every nested null as sent.
+The ids are the router's render under the encoder revision ``recipe_parity``
+(``meta.encoder_revision``, the serving router ``admission.encoder_revisions``): this fork's
+recipe-parity tree (#103, #117, #121), so a forced chat-mode tool call's ids list only the
+named tool and end inside the opened calls block (``encoding_dsv41.TOOL_CALLS_PREFILL``).
+
+The handler is the real ``OpenAIServingChat`` configured as box-c runs it (args and env
+from the router repo's records: ``--reasoning-parser auto --tool-call-parser auto``,
+which resolve to ``deepseek-v41`` / ``deepseekv41`` on DeepSeek-V4.1, as box-c's
+``/server_info`` reports; no ``--default-chat-template-kwargs``; no
+``SGLANG_DEFAULT_THINKING`` and no ``SGLANG_DSV41_REASONING_EFFORT`` in its env), with
+the real ``TemplateManager`` and the real pinned DeepSeek-V4.1 vocabulary. That
+vocabulary (``tokenizer.json`` of ``deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash`` at
+``dba1be0a40aa45a94ad051997016db3960a90277``, sha256 pinned below) is read from
+``SBR_DSV41_TOKENIZER_JSON`` (the serving router's
+``ops/tokenizers/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash.json`` is that file), else downloaded
+from the Hugging Face hub at that revision; a different file fails the run.
+
+What it shows (counts are this fixture's):
+
+* the messages path renders the router's ids for every body (167 of 167), and for the
+  76 recorded ones the count is box-c's own ``prompt_tokens``;
+* for the 26 tool-member null bodies the engine renders the router's ids from the body
+  the client sent as well as from the router's egress body;
+* the ``input_ids`` path builds a ``GenerateReqInput`` equal to the messages path's in
+  every field, ``require_reasoning`` and the tool-call constraint included, except one:
+  ``sampling_params["stop"]`` is ``[]`` where the messages path leaves ``None`` (a body
+  without ``stop``). ``SamplingParams.normalize`` maps both to ``[]``, so the scheduler's
+  sampling state is identical. The 4 forced chat-mode tool calls are the router's own
+  exception: it posts them on the messages path, never as ids (the serving router
+  ``render.rs`` ``prefills_tool_calls``, ``flight.rs`` ``ids_send_or_messages``), because
+  only there does the engine know its prompt opened the calls block; their ids-path
+  ``structural_tag``, which opens the block again, is no parity claim;
+* the request object the output parsers read afterwards is identical;
+* the ``input_ids`` path never calls the tokenizer's ``encode``;
+* the engine does not check the ids against the messages: a wrong id goes through;
+* the body the router actually posts carries a one-message placeholder,
+  ``[{"role":"user","content":""}]``, where ``messages`` was (#10070: the prompt crosses the
+  wire once, as ids). The engine reads nothing of ``messages`` that varies on the
+  ``input_ids`` path, and the router's canary compares ids, not messages, so the last
+  section of this file is the guard: with the placeholder, every body builds the same
+  ``GenerateReqInput`` (167 of 167), the same request for the parsers to read, the same
+  validation verdict, the same non-streaming response and the same stream (reasoning and
+  tool-call parse, on a scripted answer) as with the real messages; the three reads the
+  router keeps the real messages for (``continue_final_message``, message-level
+  ``tools``, a non-text part) differ, so the comparison does see a read when there is one;
+* the deployment knobs the router cannot see: ``SGLANG_DEFAULT_THINKING`` and a
+  ``default_chat_template_kwargs`` ``thinking`` change the engine's render only for a
+  body that does not spell ``chat_template_kwargs.thinking`` (1 of 167 here), and a
+  server-side default effort (``default_chat_template_kwargs.reasoning_effort`` or
+  ``SGLANG_DSV41_REASONING_EFFORT`` other than ``high``) changes it for none: the router
+  posts every thinking turn's numeric budget as ``chat_template_kwargs.reasoning_effort``
+  (43 of 167), and without it the default would change the 38 thinking bodies the client
+  gave no effort. box-c sets none of them; on the ``input_ids`` path the engine would serve
+  the router's render regardless;
+* on ``tool_choice`` "none" (2 bodies with tools) the router's render, like upstream's
+  (#43006), lists no tools.
+
+Images (part 0034, the last section, from its own fixture
+``fixtures/chat_input_ids_dsv41_images.jsonl.gz``): a DeepSeek-V4.1 prompt holds one image
+placeholder id per image and the multimodal processor expands each from the images in
+``messages``. The ``input_ids`` branch used to drop them (``image_data=None``), so ids and
+images were served as bare placeholders with no vision features. With 0034 the branch reads
+the images from ``messages`` as the messages path does, so a messages array reduced to its
+image parts is enough; the section shows that for bodies with an image in a user message, in
+a tool result, several in one message, tool results answered out of call order, five image
+formats, planner extremes and a body over the router's 642,000-token exact gate.
+"""
